<p>Added spoilers/easter eggs to the author post, to clarify some of the more obscure references and jokes.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">FOR&nbsp;TRANSLATORS:</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Links to the three pages:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/fragment:scp-4028-1">Page 1</a></li>
<li><a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/fragment:scp-4028-2">Page 2</a></li>
<li><a href="http://www.scp-wiki.net/fragment:scp-4028-3">Page 3</a></li>
</ul>
<p>One note I'd like to make regarding the various altered works on the first page:</p>
<p>Pretty much all the fiction I selected is fiction relevant to an English-speaking audience. That being said, please feel <em>more than free</em> to replace some of these entries with fiction relevant to the audience you're writing it for. For example, if you translate this into Chinese, feel free to replace (or add) entries where Don Quixote alters texts more relevant to a Chinese audience &#8212; etc. There are only a few things to keep in mind:</p>
<ul>
<li>The joke with all these texts is that each story amounts to some variation of &quot;Quixote shows up, strikes evil down, extols knightly virtues, and everyone lives happily ever after&quot;. The only exception is <em>The Raven</em>, because I couldn't imagine <em>The Raven</em>'s narrator <em>not</em> getting into a fistfight with Quixote over who is more beautiful (Lenore or Dulcinea).</li>
<li>Quixote presumes giants, wizards, serpents, and other 'creatures of sin' are trafficking with the Devil, and must be struck down. This is <em>especially</em> true with giants. In <em>Harry Potter</em>, Hagrid is a good giant &#8212; but when Quixote arrives, he is immediately struck down &#8212; because in Quixote's stories, there are no such things as 'good giants'.</li>
<li>The texts should occur in the order of earliest to oldest publication dates (IE, Quixote is 'moving forward' in time). Mind you, republished texts (like a copy of <em>A Christmas Carol</em> republished in 1996) count as 'moving forward' in time.</li>
<li>I would suggest you keep the entries short, and limit their number to seven; this is not required, though &#8212; and I'm more than comfortable just trusting translators to do their own thing for their own audience.</li>
</ul>
<p>Finally: I imagine I speak for everyone here on the English-speaking side of the SCP-wiki when I say that we are <em><strong>immensely</strong></em> appreciative of all the hard work you do translating these pieces. Thank you!</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

